      <section class="prose" aria-labelledby="why">
        <p class="eyebrow">Why this book</p>
        <h2 id="why">The book data engineers keep recommending</h2>
        <p>
          DDIA explains how data systems actually work underneath: storage engines,
          replication, partitioning, transactions, and batch and stream processing. It
          doesn't teach any single tool. It teaches the ideas every tool is built from,
          which makes it easier to judge a new database than reading its marketing page.
        </p>
        <p>
          I'm reading the second edition, updated for cloud-native systems, and writing up
          each chapter as I go. Writing forces me to check that I actually understood it.
        </p>
      </section>

